WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
- Support all of you in other ways

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.520
with a daily class schedules,
to kind of approximate,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.000
keep the learning going
on during the closers,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.500
webinars for teachers and parents,

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.680
and also this homeroom is really

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.000
just a way to stay connected.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.210
Talk to interesting people
about interesting topics

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:15.830
and answer any questions you have.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.290
And so over the course
of this conversation,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.860
and I have a very exciting guest today,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.740
I'll give him a proper
introduction in a few minutes.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:24.740
I encourage you to put any
questions you might have

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.940
on Facebook or on YouTube,

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.690
and our team is going
to be looking at them,

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.870
and getting to as many
of them as possible.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.690
I will give my standard announcement,

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:34.860
Khan Academy just as a
reminder is a not for profit,

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.460
we only exist because of
philanthropic donations.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.170
We were running at a deficit
even before the COVID crisis,

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.290
and you could imagine
now that our traffic,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:44.890
our usage is almost three
X of what it typically is,

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.430
registrations are five to 10
X of what they typically are,

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.470
and we're trying to accelerate
a bunch of programs,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.760
our costs have gone up even higher.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.670
And so if you're in a position to so,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.500
please think about making a donation.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.810
I wanna give a special shout
out to several corporations

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.960
that have stepped up in
just the last few weeks

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.330
to help with this effort
including Bank of America,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:08.330
AT&amp;T, Novartis, Fastly and Google.org.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.690
So thanks all of them, and
for our longtime supporters,

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.960
obviously, many foundations
and corporations,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.070
but we do need more help, so if you can,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.770
please think about donating.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.310
So with that, I'd like to
introduce our next guest

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.660
who is actually been playing
a huge role in Khan Academy,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.220
even existing, he's been a
supporter of Khan Academy

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.170
from really the early
years, Jorge Paulo Lemann.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.410
Nice to see you, Jorge Paulo.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.223
- Hi, how are you Sal?

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.990
- I'm doing all right, and you know,

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:41.010
I thought there's a lot of
interesting things for us

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.220
to talk about over the course of this con,

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.940
over the course of the
next 30 minutes or so.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.260
You've done very well and
from a business point of view,

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.290
you've been a very active philanthropist.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.830
I think you have some very
interesting life stories.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.430
We have some young, a lot of young people

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.260
who watch this Live stream
who are really just trying

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.980
to figure out how to
navigate their own lives,

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.843
so maybe that's a good place to start,

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.540
you know, I was reading some of your bio,

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.930
and obviously, we've known
each other for many years now,

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.220
but talk a little bit
about your early start,

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.810
you know, it sounds like
you're a very good student.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.280
You're an amazing tennis player,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.890
and then you got to college,

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:17.890
and it sounded like you didn't have the,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.870
you weren't a fish in
water initially,(laughs)

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:23.943
you had some hard times.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.107
- So I went to college
when I was very young, 17.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.770
I had never been to the
United States before.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:40.940
And I was basically a
surfer and a tennis player.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.610
And college was tough in the beginning,

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.030
I wasn't used to studying,
I wasn't used to reading,

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.713
so it was very tough
but I made it through,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.720
and adapting that first year was one

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.897
of the many crises I sort
of went through in life.

00:03:02.897 --> 00:03:05.480
I was making a list of all the crises

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.880
I've gone through in the last 80 years.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.050
And I guess it started
when I was born five days

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.510
before the Second World War.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.280
So when even when I was four or five,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.710
I remember to my family,
listening to bank,

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:24.590
Voice of America and finding
out what was going on the war.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.430
And so every sort of eight to 10 years,

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:32.386
there is some sort of
a crisis that comes up,

00:03:32.386 --> 00:03:37.060
and you really learn
how to live with them,

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:38.850
you learn how to adapt.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.920
And each one of the crises
I think I've come out

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.630
a better informed person
or better prepared person,

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.160
or like that.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.040
This crisis we're now undergoing

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.170
is one of the toughest ones,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.640
but you know, we'll make it out of it,

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.680
and I'm trying to figure out
what are gonna be the learnings

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.650
and how can I come out of
it better than I was before,

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:05.043
so this is one more crises action.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.730
- Now, that's a good
way to think about it.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.330
And you have more experience
to draw from than I do,

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.470
but it does seem like it feels
around roughly every decade,

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:15.330
something comes up either personally,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.920
or in the macro picture as a whole.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.440
But I wanna double
click on what you talked

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:21.420
about your early life,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.050
because I think there's so many students

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.560
who even before the COVID crisis

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.800
were feeling a little bit lost.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.210
They feel a hunger to make something out

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:30.870
of their life, have a purpose,

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.260
but sometimes don't know what
it is, how to discover it.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.690
And then obviously, in times of crisis,

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.870
like now things can get
even more difficult.

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:39.710
So in those early years as you mentioned;

00:04:39.710 --> 00:04:40.840
you're coming from Brazil,

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.470
you're a freshman in Boston,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:44.760
you didn't like the weather,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.130
you were having trouble getting used

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.140
to just even how things worked.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:49.810
What were you telling inside of your head,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.210
or what do you think it was the traits,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.860
or the muscles that you
had that allowed you

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.560
to persevere through that
and not essentially give up?

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:03.600
- Well, my family
thought it was important.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:06.003
I actually wanted to quit Harvard,

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.677
but you know, my family kept saying,

00:05:09.677 --> 00:05:13.837
"Ah, ah, Harvard is so important then,

00:05:13.837 --> 00:05:17.210
"get it done and get it
over with, et cetera,"

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.043
and like that.

00:05:18.043 --> 00:05:21.540
So after my first year at Harvard

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.260
where I flunked a lot of courses

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.270
and almost got suspended and like that.

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:31.640
I decided, I made a very stupid decision,

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:34.380
I decided I would go
back and I would finish

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.190
the next three years in two years.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:42.190
So I sort of buckled down, took
twice the number of courses,

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.660
I should normally be taking,

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:48.890
and so I got out of Harvard and I passed,

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:51.660
but (chuckles) I didn't really learn

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.550
as much as I could have learned,

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.260
or as much as I should have learned.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:00.113
I was just there to get it
over and done with et cetera.

00:06:01.248 --> 00:06:05.483
Not only did it later
that it became clear to me

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:10.150
how much more I could have
learned if I had taken more time.

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.210
If I had socialized a bit more.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.370
If I had met a few more of the professors

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.137
that were teaching the
courses, and things like that.

00:06:17.137 --> 00:06:20.880
And basically I was there
to get it done with,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.310
and get out of there.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.310
And so it wasn't a good decision,
but that's what happened.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:33.190
And so I got out, and then
I went to Europe to work

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:38.420
for a bank in Europe.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:42.280
And I worked there for about seven months

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:44.423
in this bank and it was very, very boring.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:47.522
And I always played a lot of tennis

00:06:47.522 --> 00:06:52.522
and all my grandparents are
Swiss, my parents are Swiss.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.470
So I have a Swiss passport,

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:58.320
so I got invited to play the
Swiss nationals in tennis

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.630
and I won the Swiss nationals,

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:05.370
which sort of surprised
everybody including myself.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.707
- You say that very nonchalantly?

00:07:06.707 --> 00:07:08.680
"Yeah, I got invited and I won,

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.930
Swiss nationals." (laughs)
(Jorge mumbles)

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.190
- And then they invited me to play tennis

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:17.940
for the Swiss national tennis
team Davis Cup and all that.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.150
I was then 21 and I thought
that was much better

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.150
than working in a bank.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:28.070
And so I did that and I played
Wimbledon and Roland-Garros,

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.340
and all the big tournaments in Europe,

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.850
and Davis Cup and I like that.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.223
Meanwhile, my family
was sort of wondering,

00:07:37.407 --> 00:07:38.967
"Is this guy just gonna play tennis,

00:07:38.967 --> 00:07:40.917
"or is he gonna do something else, or?"

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.890
Eventually, I came back to Brazil

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:46.780
and started working, et cetera,

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.693
but that was sort of my early start.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:51.490
- And then Jorge.
- And then --

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:52.423
- Oh, go ahead.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.523
- Yeah, and then when
I came back to Brazil,

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:04.467
I joined all these Ivy Leaguers guys

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:09.320
from the Harvard Business
School, Princeton, and like that,

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:12.350
and who would set up a finance company.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.930
And I thought, you know,
it looked very good,

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:18.600
and all of them had graduated
good Ivy Leaguegy schools

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.030
and like that.

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:23.550
Well, the result of that was
we went broke in three years,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:28.080
so that was a very traumatic experience.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.550
So you know, crises are always there,

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:34.560
and you learn something
in every one of them,

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.633
and hopefully you come out better.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:40.930
- And you know, in hindsight
these are fun stories,

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:43.760
because, you know, many of
us know where you ended up,

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.940
but at the time, what
were you feeling were you,

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.207
was it like a crisis of
confidence, were you just like,

00:08:49.207 --> 00:08:50.930
"I'm not going to be able to
make something out of this,"

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.130
or were you pretty, you know,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.717
how did you pick up the pieces and say,

00:08:54.717 --> 00:08:56.000
"No, I'm gonna keep working on this,"

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.950
and especially keep working in finance.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:04.560
- I'd be pressed for a while,

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.400
and you know, I graduated from Harvard,

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.123
I thought I was pretty good,

00:09:13.355 --> 00:09:16.307
and then go broke at 26 et cetera.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.540
Having just gotten married

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:22.113
like that was not a good experience.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:29.060
I was invited by a few
large organizations to go

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:30.740
and work for them.

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:35.740
And I didn't like that and
I liked the liberty of being

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:42.650
in a smaller company, being
more of an owner like that.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:46.470
So I didn't join any of
the larger organizations,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:51.470
and actually joined the small
brokerage house in Brazil,

00:09:52.002 --> 00:09:55.977
and then that it went reasonably well.

00:09:55.977 --> 00:09:58.540
But I really wanted to run my show.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.600
I didn't wanna be part
of somebody else's show

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:02.433
and like that.

00:10:02.433 --> 00:10:05.300
So after a few years of doing that,

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:09.450
I bought my own brokerage
company and set up

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:13.143
what became a successful
investment bank later.

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:16.383
- And what I know

00:10:16.383 --> 00:10:17.789
what tech ways would you have?
- I think my (mumbles).

00:10:17.789 --> 00:10:21.750
- Go ahead.
- I think my playing tennis

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.950
and you know, when you play tennis

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:28.120
you don't win all the sets,
or all the games you lose,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:31.800
and so that sort of taught
me that if you don't do well,

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.250
you can do better the next time,

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.270
you can practice more,

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:38.010
or you can figure things
out better like that.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.070
So that's been with me all my life,

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:43.290
I think always trying to do better

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:45.053
and to learn from your mistakes.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.910
And boy, do you learn a lot
from your mistakes. (chuckles)

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:51.440
- Now, this is the time we learn the most,

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:52.800
and there's two questions from YouTube

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.375
that I think are related to this one,

00:10:54.375 --> 00:10:56.217
Marcella Carter asked,

00:10:56.217 --> 00:10:57.860
"What was your biggest lesson in life?"

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:01.457
And from YouTube, Matt Gray asks,

00:11:01.457 --> 00:11:03.420
"Who was the most influential
person in your life?"

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:04.570
So when you're going through this journey,

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:05.990
I'm sure there's a lot of actors there,

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:07.630
but I guess, what advice would you give

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:10.680
to a young Jorge Paulo
or someone like you,

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.770
who's you know, in that position now,

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:15.330
and then who are your influences,

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:18.233
who kind of got you, kept
you on the trajectory?

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:22.860
- Well, you know, when I learned

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.050
that it took me a while to learn.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.270
I was a tennis player, a surfer,

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.770
and then went to the financial market,

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:36.180
and was basically a trader
in the financial market,

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.910
so all of those things basically

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:42.350
made me a short term thinker,

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:45.600
I didn't think very far ahead.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.600
And only with time and with
some of the mistakes I made,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.640
that I learned that you know,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.620
what's gonna happen in 24
hours, or in the next week,

00:11:56.620 --> 00:12:00.690
or next month is not
really that important.

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:02.700
And you gotta figure
out where you're going

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:05.910
for a longer period of time.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:10.910
And so as time went by my
thinking became much more

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.690
long term oriented.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:17.700
And it took me a while
to learn that and then,

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:21.240
you know, it sorta clicked
and some of the things

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.000
I began doing when I was already 40,

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:30.000
or getting close to 50 were
really long term ideas,

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:32.360
and they actually turned out much better

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:35.920
than in my trading days
when I was thinking

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:39.170
of the next week, or 24
hours, or things like that.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.323
In terms of the most influential person,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.580
my father died when I was 13.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:51.550
My mother was a very strong personality.

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:55.427
Yesterday was Mother's Day
so I thought a lot about her.

00:12:55.427 --> 00:13:00.090
And she was very influential,
very strong personality.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:03.720
I only had a sister who
was 10 years older than me.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:05.440
So I was sort of like an only son,

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.880
and my mother and I were very close.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.880
She was very ambitious for
me, very loving and also,

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:18.030
but gave me a lot of liberty.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:21.370
She trusted me entirely
I could sort of go out

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:24.960
and do anything I wanted on my own,

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.030
and that helped me a lot.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:31.470
And figuring, going through experiences,

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.100
which were not necessarily
always good or like that,

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:37.370
but I think the liberty she gave me,

00:13:37.370 --> 00:13:40.920
and the love she showed in
backing me up was very important

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:44.163
in everything else that
I was able to do later.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:48.630
- Now that's super powerful.

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:51.100
And you know, now you mentioned
that long term thinking

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.930
which I agree with you,
you don't see enough of it,

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.290
and I'm guessing you'd
pinpoint that as one

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:59.840
of your differentiators and
what allowed you to do well.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:03.410
You know, putting that
same hat on later in life,

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.070
you've become very active in philanthropy

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.810
and that's how we connected
almost 10 years ago now,

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:10.330
eight, nine, 10 years ago.

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:14.100
What got you so active in philanthropy?

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.560
You know, a lot of ways for those of you,

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:16.640
for those who don't know, you're one

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.450
of the most active
philanthropists in Brazil,

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:20.910
especially with the focus on education,

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:23.573
what got you interested in this problem?

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:30.120
- When I was building up our
brokerage company in Brazil,

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:34.290
I had no competitive advantage
in terms of having a name

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:35.910
or having money.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:38.610
And I figured out that the only way

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.350
I could really be competitive would be

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:45.490
if I hired and attracted
the best possible people

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.200
to work with me.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:51.000
So I made a big effort in
looking for these people.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.420
I would interview hundreds
of candidates per year,

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.020
and pick the best even when we were hiring

00:14:57.020 --> 00:14:58.883
10 or 15 people per year.

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:03.110
That became very important for us

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.233
in building up the brokerage business,

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:12.090
and in doing that, I met a lot
of really spectacular people

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:18.180
who had not had the proper education,

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.550
or had not been trained
properly, and like that,

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:23.490
they were pretty exceptional.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:28.490
And so I figured that, besides
trying to improve people

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:31.730
who were working with me,

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:34.780
and making, giving them
a better education,

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:37.630
maybe I could do it for other people.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.720
So I set up these foundations

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.340
which basically give scholarships

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:46.330
to people who wanna study
abroad at that point,

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:49.240
there were just very few scholarship

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.363
for people wanting to study abroad.

00:15:53.428 --> 00:15:56.042
And so this the foundation
which was called

00:15:56.042 --> 00:15:58.540
(speaks in foreign language) study

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:03.410
became quite important,
and over the years,

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:08.410
we have sent many
Brazilians to US colleges

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:12.790
and they've come back to Brazil,

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:15.560
they've built businesses up.

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:18.040
Lately, I've been giving
a lot of scholarships

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:21.400
to people who wanna come back
and go into public service

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:22.860
or to government.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:25.520
I figured we got enough good businessmen,

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:28.780
and we need better people in government

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:30.920
and in the public sector.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:35.920
So that's how I sort of got
going in terms of trying

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:38.210
to get people more educated,

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:43.030
or give them worthwhile
experiences by studying abroad.

00:16:43.030 --> 00:16:46.440
And then we evolved from that,

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.920
we evolved to trying to improve education

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:51.683
in Brazil in general.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:57.853
Brazil scores very badly in
all the international test,

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:00.830
we should do better.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:04.620
I'm a big believer that
countries do as well

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:06.663
as they have educated their people,

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:11.210
so for Brazil to do well in the future,

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:16.190
we have to educate more people
and educate them better.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:19.710
So my foundation now
beyond giving scholarships

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:21.480
to people wanting to study abroad,

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:24.670
we have teacher training courses.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:26.650
We work in partnership with you

00:17:26.650 --> 00:17:31.387
so that people can learn more online.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:37.290
We try to work with governments in terms

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:41.580
of improving their teaching
standards like that.

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:43.870
We give courses to school principals

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:46.140
so that they can manage
the schools better,

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:49.823
and we make a variety of efforts.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:55.870
Recently, there is one small
city in Brazil called Sobral.

00:17:57.525 --> 00:18:00.510
It's a very poor city in the northeast,

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:04.970
but for the last 10 years
it has scored really high

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:08.693
in the national test of how
public schools are doing.

00:18:09.650 --> 00:18:13.010
And we have now hired some of the people

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:17.550
that set up this system in
the city of 200,000 people,

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:21.310
and we're trying to
duplicate what they have done

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:25.561
in other small cities
of less than 200,000.

00:18:25.561 --> 00:18:29.580
And it's much easier to
introduce education systems

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:31.623
in small cities than in big cities.

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:35.320
And so it's something very practical.

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:39.880
They were successful, and we
are now in another 25 cities,

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:44.680
and if that works, we will
duplicate it to 50 cities.

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:46.623
So we have a lot of things going on,

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:49.840
besides what we do with the Khan Academy

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:52.130
which I think is really important.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:56.530
I think, you know, online
learning has a big future

00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:59.620
in Brazil and in poor countries.

00:18:59.620 --> 00:19:04.090
I think being able to
access online learning

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:06.530
in poor countries is
really more important,

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:09.810
because it gives you a
chance to reach the people

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:11.980
who really don't have a
chance to go to school,

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:15.940
or have access to terrific
teachers or like that.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:20.940
So anyway, that's, all I do
is a little drop in the bucket

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:25.530
of (chuckles) what Brazil
needs to improve education.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:28.980
But you know, I'm trying,
I'm trying to do my best.

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:31.550
- Now well, I think you're
being slightly modest there,

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:33.450
but you know, there's a
question that from YouTube

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:35.300
that Susanna Garcia Dominguez is asking,

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:37.770
and this is actually something
I've always wondered,

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:39.810
actually how did you
learn about Khan Academy,

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:40.880
how did it sharpen your radar?

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:43.860
Because you were one of the
earliest philanthropists

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:45.700
to really reach out to us,

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.463
I mean this was back in 2010 or 2011.

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:54.900
- We were reading about the Khan Academy,

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:58.800
and I said, "Gosh, this
makes so much sense."

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:02.630
And I read a few things
that you had written,

00:20:02.630 --> 00:20:05.120
and that you had spoken.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:08.060
And I thought it made so much sense.

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:12.160
And you know, so I guess
we tried to establish

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:14.000
some contact with you.

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.290
And then what happened was

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:22.683
the president of Brazil
called me up once and said,

00:20:22.683 --> 00:20:25.497
"Do you know this guy Sal Khan?

00:20:25.497 --> 00:20:27.827
"I think he's very interesting.

00:20:27.827 --> 00:20:30.170
"Can you get him to come to Brazil?"

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.670
I said, "I don't know but I can try."

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:37.670
And so we actually, we
were already talking

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:39.777
but then we said, "Sal, don't
you want to come down here,

00:20:39.777 --> 00:20:41.900
"the president of Brazil
wants to talk to you."

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:45.760
And so you came down and, you know,

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:48.997
and talked to the president and like that,

00:20:48.997 --> 00:20:51.920
and I got to know you a little bit better

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:55.520
and that's how we sort
of got going. (chuckles)

00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:57.000
- Now, I tell the story a lot.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:58.370
It's actually those early days,

00:20:58.370 --> 00:20:59.960
I mean, for those who don't know,

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:02.700
I mean back in 2010, I
think Khan Academy was

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:04.380
about an eight person organization.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:06.710
We were above a Chinese tea store

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:08.400
in downtown Mountain View here.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:11.390
And it might see my I have
an extroverted nature,

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:12.670
but I'm actually very introverted,

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:14.740
so I really enjoy kind of being by myself

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:16.630
and I think I was recording
a video or something,

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:17.677
and someone said,

00:21:17.677 --> 00:21:20.497
"Hey, you know, we have
this delegation from Brazil,

00:21:20.497 --> 00:21:21.457
"that is here ," and as like,

00:21:21.457 --> 00:21:22.870
"Oh, but I need to finish this video."

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:24.660
And I was kind of in a
grumpy mood, (chuckles)

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:26.920
because I wanted to finish
like an organic chemistry video

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:29.440
or something, and then we
met and I remember that room,

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:31.760
it was like, there was like
12 of us packed into a room

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:34.080
that could probably fit five people.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:36.300
But who would have thought,
I remember it all, you know,

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:38.200
we were very intrigued by the notion

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:39.460
of being able to work in Brazil,

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:42.490
but it felt like we were
small at that point,

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:44.680
but y'all were really pulling us in.

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:47.510
And I have to say, if not for that push,

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:49.950
then and then obviously,
we started working together

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:51.250
over the next few years.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:54.260
I don't think, you know
now, Khan Academy in Brazil

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:56.730
is the most built out Khan Academy outside

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.300
of the United States,
and it wouldn't be if not

00:21:59.300 --> 00:22:02.230
because of that first meeting
in the (mumbles) desire.

00:22:02.230 --> 00:22:04.400
And y'all have been
supporters, not just in Brazil,

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:06.430
but you know, Khan Academy globally,

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:08.620
so that was one of those meetings

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:11.570
that are definitely have been
logged in my memory bank.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:15.840
And then obviously had a
wonderful trip to Brazil.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.250
Well, one question from YouTube,

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:19.150
actually, there's two questions,

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:20.230
I think they're related,

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:24.677
Josie Mubarak asks, "What
advice would you give

00:22:24.677 --> 00:22:26.080
"to graduates?

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:28.217
And when you related that
Nikita Govender asks,

00:22:28.217 --> 00:22:29.940
"How do you build great
leadership skills?"

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.800
And I'll add to that, you mentioned,

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:34.800
like you realize the success of your orgs,

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:36.070
were hiring the right people,

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:38.490
and maybe training the right people.

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:42.810
What do you look for, like
are there signs that you see

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:43.867
in young people that you say,

00:22:43.867 --> 00:22:45.637
"Hey, that person is onto something.

00:22:45.637 --> 00:22:47.800
"This is a person I want to
invest in and work with."

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:49.587
And there's other signs
you've seen people like,

00:22:49.587 --> 00:22:52.787
"Ah, not so sure, I
think I'll pass on them."

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:58.520
- I don't think there's
any thing very special

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:00.640
the way we went about hiring people

00:23:00.640 --> 00:23:05.640
and you know, first thing I learned,

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:09.310
or I learned in my Ivy League Partnership,

00:23:09.310 --> 00:23:12.580
which didn't work out
is to run a business,

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:15.320
or to run anything you have
to have different types.

00:23:15.320 --> 00:23:19.270
So if everybody's the same
it won't work very well.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:20.970
So you know, I made an effort

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:23.840
to have a different points of view.

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:28.620
And we like people who get things done,

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:33.620
people who will work as a
team but also, you know,

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:37.213
will individually try to
get things moving or going.

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:43.330
We don't tolerate bad ethics at all.

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:45.170
I mean, there are some
people who are brilliant

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:48.320
but their ethics aren't
good, and if you know,

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:50.240
that doesn't work in an organization.

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:54.150
So if there's any sign of
ethical misbehavior like that,

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:55.393
we don't tolerate it.

00:23:56.320 --> 00:24:01.320
And basically, we'd like
giving people experiences

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:06.450
that they, to test them,

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:10.210
and the ones that do well,
we promote very fast.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:14.053
The ones that don't do well
stay behind, et cetera.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:18.510
So you know, in our organizations,

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:23.510
now we have over 200,000
employees sort of,

00:24:23.710 --> 00:24:28.710
and we always know who are,
who is on a fast track.

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:33.230
Every organization of ours

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:38.230
on a yearly basis looks at
everybody who's on a fast track,

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:42.600
and figures out what they should
be doing for these people.

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:46.200
So we're always trying to
pick the exceptional ones,

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:49.220
and giving them a chance
to do something bigger.

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:54.203
And so that's how it has basically worked.

00:24:56.950 --> 00:25:01.380
You know, I think like
I say you learn a lot

00:25:01.380 --> 00:25:06.380
from your mistakes, so I'm
talking about what worked well.

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:10.310
And maybe I should talk a little bit

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:12.283
about what didn't work well.

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:18.170
Over the last five years
our companies have not done

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:24.330
as well as they did the
first 25 years, like that.

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:29.330
And so like I said, we've
always been very people focused,

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:34.100
and always focused on
people who were ambitious,

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:39.100
wanna get things done,
cut costs, look at goals,

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:43.910
and have very clear goals and like that.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:48.530
We missed down a little
bit in terms of the world

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:51.690
becoming much more consumer centric,

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:56.690
in terms of the clients and
public having much more,

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:00.330
many more choices of what
they want to consume.

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:02.940
We're basically a
consumer goods businesses,

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:07.940
and so we didn't pay enough
attention to what our buyers,

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.240
or what our clients really wanted.

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:16.520
And at the same time we did not use

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:19.750
what is available nowadays in terms

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:21.870
of artificial intelligence to know more

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:24.300
about our clients and where they are,

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:26.630
and what they want and like that.

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:30.757
And our culture was a
culture of very much,

00:26:30.757 --> 00:26:33.367
"We gotta reach the goals,
we gotta produce more,

00:26:33.367 --> 00:26:36.050
"we got to produce cheaply and like that."

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:37.630
But we missed out a little bit

00:26:37.630 --> 00:26:40.350
on what the client really wants,

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.140
and figuring out what he really wants

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:48.000
with the modern techniques
which are available.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:52.710
So we're catching up now,
we're going in that direction.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:56.340
But you know, you have
to have good people,

00:26:56.340 --> 00:27:01.050
and you have to have good
people who are up to date

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.660
on what is going on in the world.

00:27:03.660 --> 00:27:06.570
And we didn't have enough of those

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:09.290
because our culture was so successful,

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.730
and doing so well for a while,

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:14.080
that it took us a little while to realize

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:16.520
that we had to go in this direction.

00:27:16.520 --> 00:27:18.830
So we're correcting that now.
- All right and for those --

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:21.030
And for those of you who
are watching, you know,

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:22.800
Jorge Paulo, I think very intentionally

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:27.260
keeps a fairly low profile,
but some of these organizations

00:27:27.260 --> 00:27:29.780
include things like InBev, and you know,

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:32.280
Anheuser Busch, Burger King,

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:34.820
so very, very large global brands

00:27:34.820 --> 00:27:36.760
that folks might be familiar with.

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:39.950
I mean, given your lens
on the global economy,

00:27:39.950 --> 00:27:41.810
but especially the Brazilian economy,

00:27:41.810 --> 00:27:44.060
what's your sense of the short

00:27:44.060 --> 00:27:46.080
and long term economic implications

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:47.780
of the crisis we're going through?

00:27:50.679 --> 00:27:52.670
- I think it's, like I said,

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:55.020
I have been through various crises,

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:59.357
and this one looks like
it's gonna last longer.

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:05.900
And it's gonna be tough
on the emerging markets,

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:12.300
'cause the emerging markets
really lack the organization

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:15.560
to figure out who was
sick, who isn't sick,

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:17.770
what to do about it.

00:28:17.770 --> 00:28:22.060
Emerging markets have slums,
people living very close

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:23.530
to one another.

00:28:23.530 --> 00:28:27.003
It's very difficult to have locked down.

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:30.790
The people need to make money,

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:35.390
and many of these people
in the slums are sellers

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:37.070
in the streets something like that,

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:40.200
They're not able to go out now
so they can't sell anything.

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:42.180
They're desperate to
get out there and sell,

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:46.460
so it's gonna be messier
in the emerging markets

00:28:46.460 --> 00:28:51.460
than in the more developed
countries which is very sad.

00:28:52.866 --> 00:28:56.390
And I think it will take
a little while longer

00:28:56.390 --> 00:28:59.950
than most people in the stock
market or financial market,

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:03.437
think that it will take,
you know, many people think,

00:29:03.437 --> 00:29:05.657
"Oh, three or four months from now things

00:29:05.657 --> 00:29:08.240
"will start moving again."

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:09.930
But yeah, they may start moving,

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:13.720
but it will take a lot longer
to things really fall into,

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:15.093
to fall in place again.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:17.670
And so what advice you know,

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:19.380
we have so many young people watching

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:20.810
and we have a lot of
questions I haven't gotten

00:29:20.810 --> 00:29:24.740
to anywhere close to even
the majority of them.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:27.240
But there's so many young
people who are always uncertain

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:28.270
about their future, what should I do?

00:29:28.270 --> 00:29:29.700
And there's a lot of
questions about your life,

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:31.090
and a lot of interest there,

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:33.730
but especially in a time
of uncertainty like this,

00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:36.233
what advice would you have for them?

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:43.760
- Don't give up, hang on,
things look pretty bad.

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:46.470
You sort of think the
world is coming to an end,

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:48.720
or everything is coming to an end,

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:53.300
but you know, eventually they
sort of write themselves,

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:56.970
and there are many
opportunities in crisis.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:30:00.560
I'm not only talking about
financial opportunities,

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:03.130
but there're a lot of
opportunities to learn,

00:30:03.130 --> 00:30:06.550
to learn different things, to
do things in a different way,

00:30:06.550 --> 00:30:08.860
to think in a different way.

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:13.860
So you know, think about
it, think about (chuckles)

00:30:14.160 --> 00:30:17.430
how you can adapt and what
you really want to make out

00:30:17.430 --> 00:30:21.950
of your life, and how this
crisis can perhaps help you

00:30:21.950 --> 00:30:24.293
and achieve what you want to achieve.

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:26.900
- Well, that's great advice.

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:29.400
Well, you know, I just wanna thank you,

00:30:29.400 --> 00:30:31.900
Jorge Paulo for I don't
think I've ever had a chance

00:30:31.900 --> 00:30:34.010
to do it, but I'll do it
in a very public setting.

00:30:34.010 --> 00:30:36.520
Khan Academy would not exist
in the way it does today,

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:39.770
if not for your support and
your foundation support.

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.130
And it for sure would not
exist in the way it exists

00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:44.940
in Brazil, if not your
your kind of, you know,

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:47.180
really pushing that in a
very, very early stage,

00:30:47.180 --> 00:30:50.070
so thank you and I agree
with everything you said.

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:54.050
It's gonna be interesting
times next few months, years,

00:30:54.050 --> 00:30:56.290
but there's problems to be solved

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:58.930
and we look forward to
partnering with you,

00:30:58.930 --> 00:31:00.940
and your team to get that solved.

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:03.853
So thank you so much
for being part of this.

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:07.800
- Thank you Sal and it's
been a real pleasure

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:11.210
to work with your
organization over the years.

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:14.070
We have a feeling that
we're getting something done

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:18.910
or improving things in
Brazil, so and the world.

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:23.433
So let's keep working and
we'll have a better world.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:25.480
- Sounds good to me.

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:28.700
- Thank you.
- Thank you so much.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:29.603
- [Jorge] Bye, bye.

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:31.533
- Bye, thank you.

00:31:31.533 --> 00:31:34.640
And so thanks, everyone
for joining as you can see,

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:36.470
we're, you know, I feel lucky.

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:38.870
I've said multiple times,
I feel very fortunate

00:31:38.870 --> 00:31:41.220
to have the role I have,
obviously I like to geek out

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:43.450
on academic stuff and
work with our team here

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:45.320
at Khan Academy making the product better,

00:31:45.320 --> 00:31:47.040
making the experience better.

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:49.380
But another joy of this job is being able

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:52.060
to have really amazing
partners like Jorge Paulo,

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:55.780
who I consider a mentor who
pushed us to do more things,

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:58.460
who allow frankly a lot of
what we do to be possible.

00:31:58.460 --> 00:32:01.100
So you know, I think I get
disproportionate credit

00:32:01.100 --> 00:32:02.350
for the work at Khan Academy.

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:05.940
It's a team of 200 full time
folks, thousands of volunteers,

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:08.740
hundreds of thousands of
folks, many of you donate,

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:12.330
and many really great
partners like Jorge Paulo

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:13.560
and his team.

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:16.180
So with that, thank you for
joining this Livestream,

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:18.940
and I look forward to seeing
all of you all tomorrow.

00:32:18.940 --> 00:32:21.165
Have a good night, afternoon, day.

00:32:21.165 --> 00:32:23.248
(laughs)

